==============================================================================
SS2  The trivial bound and the OBSERVED growth exponent
==============================================================================

  Trivial: |S(x)| <= x.  (STAR) is exactly the statement that this is
  far from sharp: S(x)/x -> 0.  The relevant quantitative question is
  the growth of the running maximum M(x) = max_{x'<=x} |S(x')|.

  log-log fit  log M(x) = alpha*log x + b   over x = 1000, 10000, 100000, 1e+06, 1e+07, 1e+08, 1e+09:
      alpha = 0.5200 +/- 0.0249        b = -0.4038
      rms residual in log M = 0.2562  (over 7 points, 6 decades)

  The same quantity normalised by the classical x^{1/2} barrier:
      x            M(x)                M(x)/sqrt(x)
      1000         30                  0.948683
      10000        73                  0.730000
      100000       282                 0.891762
      1000000      689                 0.689000
      10000000     3059                0.967341
      100000000    6454                0.645400
      1000000000   48700               1.540029

  HONEST READING OF alpha.
   * alpha is the exponent of a RUNNING MAXIMUM over 6 decades; a
     running maximum can only grow, so it is biased upward relative to
     the true pointwise size, by roughly +1/(2*#decades).
   * Values M(x)/sqrt(x) that drift upward slowly are exactly the
     x^{1/2+eps} behaviour one expects; alpha slightly above 1/2 is
     that drift, not evidence against the x^{1/2} barrier.
   * alpha strictly below 1 is the finite shadow of o(x).  It is NOT a
     proof of o(x): a finite fit cannot exclude a later excursion, and
     SS4 makes that precise.  Nothing here is a claim about (STAR).
