==============================================================================
SS3  The logarithmic average -- an unconditional THEOREM, checked
     finitely (this is the one section whose target is known)
==============================================================================

  THEOREM (Tao 2015; logarithmically averaged Chowla, fixed shift h):
      L_h(x) := (1/log x) sum_{n<=x} lambda(n)lambda(n+h)/n -> 0.
  This is unconditional and holds for every fixed h.  It does NOT give
  (STAR) and (STAR) does not give it: the weight 1/n concentrates on
  n <= x^{o(1)}, where the two statements carry no information about
  each other.  The elementary implication that does exist is one-way:
      S(t) = O(t^{1-delta}) for some delta > 0  ==>  L_h(x) -> 0,
  by Abel summation, sum_{n<=x} f(n)/n = S(x)/x + int_1^x S(t)/t^2 dt,
  whose integral converges under the hypothesis.  That hypothesis is
  UNPROVEN (it is a power saving in the correlation, essentially RH-
  strength); Tao's proof avoids it.  The identity is what makes the
  logarithmic average strictly weaker than (STAR).

  h = 2:   x            sum lambda(n)lambda(n+2)/n      L_2(x)
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------
           100000       -1.35427338                 -0.1176306913
           1000000      -1.35611730                 -0.0981590430
           10000000     -1.35631338                 -0.0841484884
           100000000    -1.35639595                 -0.0736344098
           1000000000   -1.35634567                 -0.0654503824

  h = 1:   x            sum lambda(n)lambda(n+1)/n      L_1(x)
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------
           100000       -0.83826375                 -0.0728106641
           1000000      -0.84285215                 -0.0610076728
           10000000     -0.84241629                 -0.0522652492
           100000000    -0.84248851                 -0.0457360136
           1000000000   -0.84249019                 -0.0406543156

  PRECISION.  The weighted sums are accumulated in float64 over
  n <= 1000000000.  Each term has |lambda(n)lambda(n+h)/n| <= 1/n, and the
  partial sums here are O(1); the accumulated rounding is bounded by
  (number of additions) * eps * max partial sum ~ 1e9 * 2.2e-16 * 1
  = 2.2e-7 in the worst case (in practice far less, since the terms
  are mostly tiny).  The reported digits at the 1e-3 level are safe.

  Decay fit of the requested kind, |L(x)| ~ x^{-beta} over x=100000, 1e+06, 1e+07, 1e+08, 1e+09:
      beta(h=2) = -0.0634 +/- 0.0031      beta(h=1) = -0.0631 +/- 0.0030
  A power law is the WRONG model here (see below), so beta is quoted
  as a description of the range only; it is small, i.e. the decay is
  far slower than any power, which is exactly 1/log x behaviour.

  WHAT THE THEOREM DOES AND DOES NOT PREDICT.  It predicts L_h(x) -> 0
  and nothing about the rate.  The weighted sum W_h(x) =
  sum_{n<=x} lambda(n)lambda(n+h)/n need NOT converge to 0 for the
  theorem to hold (W_h could oscillate and L_h = W_h/log x would still
  vanish).  What is CHECKED below is that (i) |L_h| decreases over the
  computed range, and (ii) it does so at the 1/log x scale, i.e.
  L_h(x)*log x = W_h(x) is nearly constant -- equivalently a
  through-origin fit |L_h| = c/log x has a small relative residual.
  Both are finite observations on 5 points; neither is a theorem.

  h=2:  fitted c in L = c/log x :  -1.35557853
