  WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT SHOW.  It shows that validity on a finite
  prefix does not imply (STAR); it does NOT show that (STAR) is false,
  because lambda+ and lambda- are not the Liouville function (they are
  not even completely multiplicative).  The correct conclusion is
  EPISTEMIC: a proof of (STAR) requires global structure (e.g. Tao's
  entropy-decrement method for the logarithmic average), never more
  numerical values.

==============================================================================
SS5  Status of the analytic route -- what is known, what is open,
     and what is finitely checkable
==============================================================================

  (a) UNCONDITIONALLY KNOWN (theorems; quoted, not reproved here):
      * Tao 2015: the logarithmically averaged two-point Chowla
        conjecture holds for every fixed shift h, h = 2 included.
        [checked finitely in SS3]
      * Matomaki-Radziwill-Tao 2015/2016: the two-point correlation
        vanishes for ALMOST ALL shifts h in a short range, and the
        logarithmically averaged statement holds for all h in short
        ranges.
      * Trivially |S(x)| <= x (SS2).

  (b) OPEN.  The sharp un-averaged statement (STAR), S(x) = o(x) for
      the single shift h = 2, is a CONJECTURE.  It is not a theorem and
      not a corollary of anything cited here.  In particular NO
      RH-conditional proof is asserted: this file makes no claim that
      RH implies (STAR).  Nor can multiplicativity be invoked: for the
      deterministic multiplicative function lambda == 1 the analogue is
      sum_{n<=x} 1 = x, not o(x).

  (c) WHY THE ELEMENTARY EULER-PRODUCT/PERRON ROUTE DOES NOT APPLY --
      a finite algebraic fact, verified numerically here.  Write
          f(n) = lambda(n)lambda(n+2),   D(s) = sum_n f(n) n^{-s}.
      The route would need f multiplicative, so that D(s) has an Euler
      product and zeta controls the shift prime by prime.  It is not:
[PASS] S5.not.multiplicative   |  f(1) = lambda(1)lambda(3) = -1 (multiplicativity would need f(1)=1), and coprime counterexamples exist: f(2)f(11) = -1*1 = -1 != f(22) = 1; f(2)f(13) = -1*-1 = 1 != f(26) = -1; f(2)f(17) = -1*1 = -1 != f(34) = 1

      s = 2 test (absolute convergence, so both sides are defined and
      every truncation is bounded):
        D(2) = sum_{n<=1000000} f(n)/n^2            = -1.12681304
        tail bound  |sum_{n>N} f(n)/n^2|       <= 2.00000000e-06
        prod_{p<=1e5} (1 + sum_k f(p^k)/p^{2k}) = 0.88759631
        tail bound for that product            <= 1.80898647
        |D(2) - product|                       = 2.01440934
[PASS] S5.no.euler.product   |  the naive Euler product differs from D(2) by 2.014409, which exceeds the sum of the truncation bounds 1.808988: f has no Euler product, so the zeta-based RH route is unavailable for this shift

  (d) WHAT IS FINITELY CHECKABLE ABOUT D(s), AND WHAT IT DOES NOT DO.
      Absolute convergence for Re s >= 1 + delta is trivial (|f| <= 1):
      the tail is bounded by sum_{n>N} n^{-1-delta} <= N^{-delta}/delta,
      an explicit bound on the discarded part.  Divergence or
      convergence at Re s <= 1 is NOT unconditional: with no Euler
      product there is no independent handle.  The one general
      implication that does hold is one-directional,
          D(s) converges on Re s > beta   ==>   S(x) = O(x^{beta+eps}),
      by partial summation, so a proven beta <= 1/2 would give (STAR).
      Nothing computes beta.  The partial sums below are a FINITE
